2026-10-12|Maximum Sum of Squares After Bitwise Operations

Question
Given a 0-indexed integer array `nums` and a positive integer `k`, repeatedly apply the following operation any number of times:
- Choose distinct indices `i` and `j`.
- Simultaneously set `nums[i] = nums[i] & nums[j]` and `nums[j] = nums[i] | nums[j]`, using the original values for both calculations.
Choose `k` elements from the final array to maximize the sum of their squares. Return the answer modulo `10^9 + 7`.

Approach
1. Count the number of set bits at each bit position across all elements of `nums`.

2. Since the operation preserves the total number of set bits at each bit position, these counts remain unchanged.

3. Construct `k` numbers greedily, one at a time.

4. For each number, examine every bit position. If that bit is still available, set it in the current number and decrease its remaining count by one.

5. This creates numbers containing as many available set bits as possible, concentrating bits into fewer numbers.

6. Calculate the square of each constructed number and add it to the answer modulo `10^9 + 7`.

7. Time complexity is O(nB + kB), where `B` is the number of bits processed.

8. Space complexity is O(B) for the bit counts.

Solution
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int maxSum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;
        const int B = 31;

        vector<int> bitCount(B, 0);

        // Count set bits at each position.
        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int b = 0; b < B; b++) {
                if (num & (1LL << b)) {
                    bitCount[b]++;
                }
            }
        }

        long long ans = 0;

        // Greedily construct k numbers with the largest possible values.
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            long long value = 0;

            for (int b = 0; b < B; b++) {
                if (bitCount[b] > 0) {
                    value |= (1LL << b);
                    bitCount[b]--;
                }
            }

            ans = (ans + value * value % MOD) % MOD;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(ans);
    }
};
